#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119587 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119773 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d1637e23-6566-5e45-af4c-b27333f882d1" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Determinant Using a Laplace Expansion Across a Row"}] :question/id #uuid "8235213e-4e88-57f6-89b0-de589ad2b38d" :question/math-academy-id "e-4418" :question/problem "Calculate\n![](images/15/15738eec7022be44c90577b931f2e8aa8446a8fc3f911260fcf468ae6ddd2d57.png)" :question/worked-solution "The $4$th row of the matrix contains three zeros, more than any other row. So using a Laplace expansion along the $4$th row, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}| & =0\\cdot {C}_{41}+0\\cdot {C}_{42}+0\\cdot {C}_{43}+(-1)\\cdot {C}_{44} \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot (-1)^{4+4}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 3\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the $3 \\times 3$ determinant by applying a Laplace expansion along the $1$st row, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 3\\end{aligned}| & =1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}|-(-1)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}0 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}|+0\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}|+|\\begin{aligned}0 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(3-8)+(0-0) \\\\  & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n![](images/ce/ce96c053abb756977f717e7130257af39307e5f663596042ddcb5b14b0153cb8.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119588 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119774 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "db6f57f3-d9f0-5ea7-a557-e729a8c0777f" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Determinant Using a Laplace Expansion Across a Column"}] :question/id #uuid "6f0a6499-03db-5833-b175-4835325d34d6" :question/math-academy-id "e-4419" :question/problem "Using a cofactor expansion along the $2$nd column, write the following determinant as a combination of $3 \\times 3$ determinants:\n\n![](images/82/82b151cbc7d19f4afa375763d0ca8eb281d76e11f2d775c068e97dbf3d40cae4.png)" :question/worked-solution "Using a Laplace expansion along the $2$nd column, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -3 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 3 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 8 & 9 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 3 & -1\\end{aligned}| & =0\\cdot {C}_{12}+3\\cdot {C}_{22}+0\\cdot {C}_{32}+0\\cdot {C}_{42} \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-1)^{2+2}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 8 & 9 \\\\ 2 & 3 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 8 & 9 \\\\ 2 & 3 & -1\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n![](images/ef/efb849ac6e240be4d0a6aac3e7f9405158b8b1bb996956b7346c02b5bee751b1.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119657 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119843 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a88f6712-2115-55ce-abe4-b3c27e2e105f" :knowledge-point/title "Applying a Laplace Expansion Across a Row"}] :question/id #uuid "5cc1443c-03cd-5f86-bcfc-b9b7f4c6a565" :question/math-academy-id "e-5182" :question/problem "Using a cofactor expansion along the $4$th row, write the following determinant as a combination of $3 \\times 3$ determinants:\n\n![](images/50/502b9464378fb5030a72918f45938184f120773e4639f3f50f071ca82fc307ce.png)" :question/worked-solution "Using a Laplace expansion along the $4$th row, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}10 & 2 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 7 & 9 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -6 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| & =(-6)\\cdot {C}_{41}+0\\cdot {C}_{42}+0\\cdot {C}_{43}+1\\cdot {C}_{44} \\\\  & =(-6)\\cdot (-1)^{4+1}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 7 & 9 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 3\\end{aligned}|+1\\cdot (-1)^{4+4}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}10 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 7 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =6\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 7 & 9 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 3\\end{aligned}|+|\\begin{aligned}10 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 7 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n![](images/a1/a1c11cfd7d1cacae0881118776eb6b38dea7b92c4f542e8158e31bbfa59274ec.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369686 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119773 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d1637e23-6566-5e45-af4c-b27333f882d1" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Determinant Using a Laplace Expansion Across a Row"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27a5c820-be5b-5082-926b-e68028730b71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369688} {:answer/id #uuid "3ea3bb4d-945d-58f9-9adb-63f707e063fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369689} {:answer/id #uuid "81a658e1-0516-5466-a149-e2eb4f1f4c21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369690} {:answer/id #uuid "f056f334-c31b-5405-870a-03cf05d84686" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369691} {:answer/id #uuid "ae5e7563-f924-5173-b2ac-acb8371abc72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369692}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ae5e7563-f924-5173-b2ac-acb8371abc72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369692} :answer-field/id #uuid "f92affe9-b7e7-55a2-9d79-b25b03dbdff1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369687}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b87ab7d2-a978-564c-941d-810575e01d02" :question/math-academy-id "q-130035" :question/problem "Compute the following determinant using a cofactor expansion along the $3$rd row:\n\n$$\n|\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}|\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using cofactor expansion along the third row, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}| & =0\\cdot {C}_{31}+2\\cdot {C}_{32}+0\\cdot {C}_{33}+0\\cdot {C}_{34} \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-1)^{3+2}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 5 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2|\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 5 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the $3\\times 3$ determinant by applying a Laplace expansion along the $1$st row, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 5 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}| & =4\\cdot {C}_{11}+0\\cdot {C}_{12}+0\\cdot {C}_{13} \\\\  & =4\\cdot (-1)^{1+1}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =4|\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =4(3\\cdot 2-(-1)\\cdot (-2)) \\\\  & =4\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n|\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}|=-2\\cdot 16=-32.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265821 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119774 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "db6f57f3-d9f0-5ea7-a557-e729a8c0777f" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Determinant Using a Laplace Expansion Across a Column"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "664a8be3-c96f-5f40-a756-b75cb919d17f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 7 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -4 & -1\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 1759218